Fragmentation in molecular graphs typically involves breaking the graph \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{G}}_{a}$$\end{document}Ga into a set of subgraphs. For each peptide structure, we annotate the indices of sidechain connection bonds and amide bonds in RDkit’s format. By cleaving these bonds, we isolate the sidechain and backbone structures. However, simply dividing peptides into sidechains and backbones may not provide sufficient information for the model to learn effectively, given the structural similarity of most amino acids (with sidechains typically connected to the α-carbon while other parts remain nearly identical). To expose more features for effective learning, we further segment the sidechains. To possibly preserve biological meaning, this is achieved using the Breaking of Retrosynthetically Interesting Chemical Substructures (BRICS) rule where applicable [59]. If BRICS cannot be further applied, the sidechain is processed using MacFrag [60]. If neither method is applicable, the amino acid remains unfragmented. The backbone is then fragmented at the amide bonds. This process results in the peptide being divided into sidechain fragments, backbone, and backbone fragments. We refer to this strategy as"Fragmentize SideChain Even Sub-Sidechain"(FraSCESS). All molecules are processed in RDKit [61].

